=========================================================================

bsecret keeps an organization's secrets on the BSV blockchain's overlay
hosts, which hold ciphertext and never a key. A secret is encrypted once
under a random content key that is committed to before anyone holds it;
the key is wrapped to the owner's own wallet and under the key of one
epoch of a group; and a vault is a chain of mined tokens that lists
every version it holds and the epoch each is keyed under. When the group
removes a member it begins a new epoch, and from the token in which the
vault acknowledges it every overlay host refuses, on the vault's chain,
anything but the re-keying or retirement of what the vault held.

Secret records are never mined. Each rides an unmined carrier
transaction whose one input spends a mined funding output, so it
verifies by SPV without paying for block space, and a mined spend of
that output retracts it at every honest host. A host that carries a
crypt is a replica, not an authority: a reader verifies everything a
host answers, a root over the vault's set makes a withheld record
visible, and the chain is an audit trail anyone verifies from block
headers alone.

